    "description": "Main Purpose Execute the pharmacy/medical warehouse storing activities in order to keep pharmacy stock above security level and following recommended conditions (temperature, humidity, etc.) under the supervision of the Medi-cal Warehouse (for OCA) or Pharmacy Supervisor, and in collaboration with the logistics team. Accountabilities Supervising material orders and dispatching in order to ensure rational use. Receiving orders and deliveries, identifying potential disparities against cargo manifest or others and reporting them, and stores materials in accordance with the system in force, in order to ensure continuous availability. It includes: Ensuring good storage conditions of all medical items in the pharmacy in accordance with pharmaceutical standards in order to ensure continuous availability. Ensuring correct fill in entries on the stock cards and software. Carrying out or participating in regular inventories including EPREP stock. Following up stock levels with regards to alarms thresholds, stock out, expiry dates and drugs to expire in the following 6 months. Preparing orders of the different consumption units (departments, wards, others), ensuring proper packaging and dispatching and reporting any anomalies or changes in consumption patterns to the Pharmacy Supervisor. Informing Pharmacy Supervisor as soon as stock reaches alert limit. Controlling warehouse facilities in order to ensure materials are kept in good keeping conditions, it includes: e.g. Monitoring temperature in the warehouse and Ensuring that products requiring cold chain are suitably stored and transports received, stored, and prepared and transported appropriately according to MSF Supply and Cold Chain protocols. Ensuring cleanliness of premises (stock pharmacy) and in close collaboration with the logistics department, maintain-ing refrigerating equipment. Controlling warehouse limited access to authorized personnel and ensuring doors and other exits are secured. Im-mediately informing the line manager of any problems arising in the course of the work, particularly with regards to damage, loss, attempted break-ins or theft in the warehouse. Ensuring that items to be quarantined (on-going quality alerts) or to be placed in quarantine zone (batch recalls, ex-pired or damaged items) are removed from the stock and safely locked. Following up stock levels with regards to alarms thresholds, stock out, expiry dates and drugs to expire in the following 6 months. Preparing reports and passing it to the supervisor. MSF Section/Context Specific Accountabilities Location: Based in Begi, West Wellega, Oromia Region, with flexibility to support mobile clinics and other MSF-supported activities according to operational needs. Under the supervision of the Project Pharmacy Manager (PPM), the Pharmacy Storekeeper is responsible for ensuring effective stock management, traceability and timely distribution of medicines and medical supplies for the Begi project, including hospital support and mobile clinic activities, in collaboration with relevant MoH counterparts. A. Pharmacy stock and warehouse management Ensure daily updating of stock cards and pharmacy management software, maintaining consistency between physical and recorded stock. Ensure appropriate stock rotation according to FEFO and FIFO principles. Participate in monthly inventories and additional stock counts as requested by the PPM, reporting and docu-menting any discrepancies. Maintain daily temperature and humidity records and immediately report any deviations from recommended storage conditions. Ensure proper documentation and traceability of all medical supplies received, issued, transferred, donated or returned. Proactively identify and report potential stock shortages, overstock, near-expiry items and abnormal consump-tion patterns, considering the project's supply and accessibility challenges. Support the monitoring and maintenance of EPREP stocks and the preparation of supplies for seasonal disease peaks and emergency interventions. Contribute to weekly and monthly pharmacy reporting. B. Mobile clinic pharmacy support Prepare and pack medicines and medical supplies according to the approved mobile clinic schedule and re-quirements. Ensure appropriate storage, transportation and protection of medical supplies during mobile clinic activities. Receive, count and reconcile unused medical supplies returned after mobile clinic activities. Collect and compile consumption data from mobile clinics and report discrepancies or unusual consumption to the PPM. Support the timely replenishment of mobile clinic medical stocks to ensure continuity of services. C. Support to Begi Hospital and MoH-supported activities Prepare and distribute medical supplies to MSF-supported hospital departments according to approved orders and distribution schedules. Maintain accurate records of medical supplies provided to MoH-supported services. Support routine stock monitoring and consumption data collection in supported hospital departments, under the guidance of the PPM. Report shortages, discrepancies, inappropriate storage conditions or unusual consumption patterns identified in supported departments. Reinforce good pharmacy storage and stock management practices in collaboration with MoH pharmacy staff, as directed by the PPM. D. Temporary dispensing support During periods of increased workload, and when requested by the supervisor, provide temporary dispensing support within the limits of professional qualifications and applicable national regulations. Dispense medicines strictly according to prescriptions issued by authorized prescribers. Ensure accurate labelling and provide patients with clear instructions on medication use. Refer unclear prescriptions, dosage concerns, potential medication errors and unavailable medicines to the au-thorized prescriber. Do not independently modify prescriptions, dosages, treatment duration or substitute prescribed medicines. Maintain appropriate dispensing and consumption records. Ensure patient confidentiality, respectful communication and adherence to MSF safeguarding principles. Dispensing support should not compromise the primary responsibilities of pharmacy stock and warehouse management.",
