    "datePosted": "2026-11-27T12:24:12.000Z",
    "description": "TERMS OF REFERENCE (TOR) Consultancy for Transcription and Translation of Participatory Disaster Risk Management (DRM) Research Data . Background Brooke Ethiopia is part of Brooke, an international non-governmental organization dedicated to improving the welfare of working horses, donkeys, and mules while enhancing the livelihoods of the people who depend on them. Founded in 1934, Brooke currently operates in several countries across Africa, Asia, and Latin America. Brooke Ethiopia was established in 2006 and currently implementing equine welfare programmes across Oromia, Amhara, Sidama, and Central Ethiopia, as well as in collaboration with veterinary and agricultural training institutions, including Jimma, Hawassa, Wollo, Bahir Dar, and Haramaya Universities. Ethiopia is home to more than 13 million equines that play a vital role in the national economy and support the livelihoods of millions of people. Working equids provide essential transport services, support agricultural production, facilitate access to markets and social services, and contribute significantly to household incomes. Despite their importance, working equids continue to face poor welfare conditions due to inadequate management practices, limited access to quality animal health services, and insufficient consideration within development and emergency response systems. Working animals and their owners are equally vulnerable to disasters such as drought, flooding, conflict, disease outbreaks, and environmental degradation. However, disaster risk management (DRM) policies and practices in many low- and middle-income countries often overlook the role and needs of working animals. This exclusion can increase the vulnerability of both animals and the communities that depend on them. To address this gap, Brooke Ethiopia is conducting a participatory study to explore perceptions, practices, challenges, and opportunities related to the inclusion of working animals in disaster risk management systems (with a focus on floods and droughts). The study aims to generate evidence that will inform policy engagement, advocacy, and capacity-building efforts at national, regional, community, and household levels. As part of the study, qualitative data will be collected from communities and key stakeholders in three flood and drought-prone contexts: Borana Zone : Drought-prone pastoralist community. Halaba Area : drought-prone mixed farming community. Adama Area : Urban and peri-urban community affected by recurrent flooding. The study will employ Participatory Action Research (PAR) approaches, particularly: World Café discussions. Participatory mapping. Focus Group Discussions (FGDs) and community reflection sessions. Brooke Ethiopia seeks to engage a qualified consulting firm, agency, individual consultant, to undertake transcription and translation of qualitative data in the form of audio files into English. Purpose of the Consultancy The purpose of this consultancy is to provide accurate, confidential transcription and English translation of Participatory Disaster Risk Management (DRM) audio recordings and field notes collected in Afaan Oromo and Amharic. The consultant will ensure all deliverables are thoroughly proofread and formatted to faithfully preserve the original meaning, local context, and participant perspectives. Scope of Work and Estimated Timeline The selected consultant or firm will transcribe, translate, and quality-assure qualitative data collected through World Café discussions and community reflection session along with accompanying field notes. Estimated Workload: Approximately 30 hours of audio recordings (comprising an estimated 10 hours in Afaan Oromo and 20 hours in Amharic) and few pages of notes Assignment Duration & Schedule: The consultancy must be completed within a one (1) month period from the date of contract signing and handover of audio files. This timeline includes bi-weekly progress check-ins and a 3-day review window for Brooke Ethiopia’s quality assurance and feedback. Deliverable Format & Confidentiality: All transcripts and English translations must be submitted in Brooke Ethiopia’s designated Microsoft Word template following agreed speaker-labeling and terminology conventions, with all raw audio and research files permanently deleted upon final acceptance. Specific Objectives The consultant will: Transcribe Qualitative Data: Produce accurate, verbatim written transcripts of all audio recordings (FGDs, World Café, and reflection sessions) and relevant field notes in their original languages (Afaan Oromo and Amharic), including clear speaker identification. Translate into English: Translate all transcribed materials into clear, professionally written English while faithfully preserving participants’ original intent, meaning, cultural context, and local pastoralist/DRM terminology. Ensure Quality Assurance: Thoroughly review, edit, and proofread all transcripts and translations for accuracy, completeness, and formatting consistency. Deliver on Schedule: Submit all final deliverables in the agreed Microsoft Word template within one month’s period. Maintain Data Confidentiality: Uphold strict data protection protocols by securely storing, handling, and deleting all audio files, transcripts, and research materials upon completion of the assignment. Expected Deliverables The consultant shall submit the following outputs to Brooke Ethiopia’s satisfaction: Verbatim Source-Language Transcripts: Complete, accurate verbatim transcripts of all assigned audio recordings and field notes in their original languages (Afaan Oromo and/or Amharic), formatted in Microsoft Word with clear speaker identification (e.g., Moderator, Participant 1, Participant 2 and etc. ) Proofread English Translations: High-quality, finalized English translations of all approved transcripts and field notes in Microsoft Word (following Brooke Ethiopia’s agreed template), faithfully preserving original meaning, cultural context, and local terminology. Brief Progress Updates: Periodic status updates (e.g., weekly email check-ins or batch trackers) detailing completed, in-progress, and pending audio files. Data Deletion Confirmation: A written confirmation (or signed non-disclosure/data destruction statement) upon final acceptance verifying that all raw audio recordings, transcripts, and research materials have been permanently deleted from the consultant’s devices and storage systems. Payment schedule Payments will be made based on the satisfactory completion and acceptance of the agreed transcription and translation deliverables as follows: 20% of the total contract value upon commencement of the assignment and receipt of the sample batch audio recordings and relevant materials required for transcription and translation. 80% of the total contract value upon submission and formal acceptance of all final transcripts and translations, provided that the deliverables meet the agreed quality standards, all requested revisions have been adequately addressed, and the assignment has been completed within the agreed timeline.",
